---
import PhaseLayout from '../../layouts/PhaseLayout.astro';
---

<PhaseLayout title="The Polymaths -- History's Greatest Minds" activeNav="polymaths">

  <div class="track-header" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
    <div class="track-title">The Polymaths -- History's Greatest Minds</div>
    <div class="track-sub">From Aristotle to Turing, these are the individuals who refused to be confined to a single domain. They connected fields, invented new ones, and reshaped the world by thinking across boundaries. Study their patterns. Absorb their methods. Become one of them.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="nav">
    <button class="tn active" onclick="show(0)">Ancient</button>
    <button class="tn" onclick="show(1)">Renaissance</button>
    <button class="tn" onclick="show(2)">Enlightenment</button>
    <button class="tn" onclick="show(3)">Modern</button>
    <button class="tn" onclick="show(4)">Contemporary</button>
    <button class="tn" onclick="show(5)">The Pattern</button>
  </div>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- PANEL 0: THE ANCIENT POLYMATHS                                -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <div class="panel active" id="p0">
    <div class="track-header">
      <div class="track-badge badge-math">~600 BCE -- 1100 CE</div>
      <div class="track-title">The Ancient Polymaths</div>
      <div class="track-sub">The original cross-domain thinkers. In an era without disciplinary boundaries, these minds moved freely between philosophy, mathematics, medicine, engineering, and poetry -- laying the foundations for every field we know today.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="pm-grid">

      <!-- ARISTOTLE -->
      <div class="polymath-entry">
        <div class="pm-header">
          <div class="pm-initials">AR</div>
          <div class="pm-info">
            <div class="pm-name">Aristotle</div>
            <div class="pm-years">384--322 BCE</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-bio">Student of Plato, tutor of Alexander the Great, and arguably the most influential intellectual in Western history. Aristotle single-handedly founded or formalized logic, biology, political science, metaphysics, ethics, and literary criticism. His works dominated European and Islamic thought for nearly two millennia.</div>
        <div class="pm-domains">
          <span class="rtag free">Logic</span>
          <span class="rtag must">Philosophy</span>
          <span class="rtag vid">Biology</span>
          <span class="rtag book">Physics</span>
          <span class="rtag free">Political Science</span>
          <span class="rtag must">Ethics</span>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-contributions">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Greatest Contributions</div>
          <ul>
            <li>Created formal logic (syllogisms) -- the foundation of all deductive reasoning and computer science</li>
            <li>Classified over 500 animal species, establishing comparative biology centuries before Darwin</li>
            <li>Wrote the <em>Nicomachean Ethics</em>, still the starting point for virtue ethics philosophy</li>
            <li>Formalized rhetoric and poetics, including the concept of catharsis in drama</li>
            <li>Founded the Lyceum, one of the first systematic research institutions</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-quote">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Key Insight</div>
          <blockquote>"The more you know, the more you realize you don't know." -- Aristotle understood that knowledge expands the frontier of ignorance, driving perpetual curiosity.</blockquote>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-reading">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Recommended Reading</div>
          <ul>
            <li><em>The Nicomachean Ethics</em> -- Aristotle (translated by Terence Irwin)</li>
            <li><em>Aristotle's Way</em> -- Edith Hall (2019)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ARCHIMEDES -->
      <div class="polymath-entry">
        <div class="pm-header">
          <div class="pm-initials">AC</div>
          <div class="pm-info">
            <div class="pm-name">Archimedes</div>
            <div class="pm-years">c. 287--212 BCE</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-bio">The greatest mathematician and engineer of antiquity. Working in Syracuse, Archimedes discovered the principles of buoyancy and the lever, approximated pi with stunning accuracy, and invented war machines that held off the Roman siege for years. He anticipated integral calculus by nearly two thousand years with his method of exhaustion.</div>
        <div class="pm-domains">
          <span class="rtag free">Mathematics</span>
          <span class="rtag must">Physics</span>
          <span class="rtag vid">Engineering</span>
          <span class="rtag book">Astronomy</span>
          <span class="rtag free">Invention</span>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-contributions">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Greatest Contributions</div>
          <ul>
            <li>Discovered the principle of buoyancy (Archimedes' Principle) while analyzing a crown's purity</li>
            <li>Proved the law of the lever and declared "Give me a place to stand and I will move the Earth"</li>
            <li>Approximated pi between 3 10/71 and 3 1/7, the best estimate for centuries</li>
            <li>Invented the Archimedes screw for raising water, still used in modern irrigation</li>
            <li>Anticipated calculus with the "method of exhaustion" to compute areas and volumes</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-quote">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Key Insight</div>
          <blockquote>"Give me a lever long enough and a fulcrum on which to place it, and I shall move the world." -- Archimedes understood leverage as both a physical and intellectual principle: the right tool amplifies everything.</blockquote>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-reading">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Recommended Reading</div>
          <ul>
            <li><em>The Works of Archimedes</em> -- edited by T. L. Heath</li>
            <li><em>Archimedes and the Roman Imagination</em> -- Mary Jaeger (2008)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- HYPATIA -->
      <div class="polymath-entry">
        <div class="pm-header">
          <div class="pm-initials">HY</div>
          <div class="pm-info">
            <div class="pm-name">Hypatia of Alexandria</div>
            <div class="pm-years">c. 350--415 CE</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-bio">The first well-documented female mathematician, astronomer, and philosopher. Head of the Neoplatonic school in Alexandria, Hypatia edited and commented on foundational mathematical texts, built astrolabes and hydrometers, and became a symbol of learning in the ancient world. Her murder by a Christian mob marked the symbolic end of classical antiquity's intellectual openness.</div>
        <div class="pm-domains">
          <span class="rtag free">Mathematics</span>
          <span class="rtag must">Astronomy</span>
          <span class="rtag vid">Philosophy</span>
          <span class="rtag book">Engineering</span>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-contributions">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Greatest Contributions</div>
          <ul>
            <li>Edited and wrote commentaries on Diophantus's <em>Arithmetica</em>, preserving key algebraic ideas</li>
            <li>Commented on Apollonius's work on conic sections, which later influenced Kepler</li>
            <li>Designed improved astrolabes for astronomical measurement</li>
            <li>Invented a graduated brass hydrometer for measuring liquid density</li>
            <li>Led Alexandria's intellectual community, teaching students from across the Mediterranean</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-quote">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Key Insight</div>
          <blockquote>"Reserve your right to think, for even to think wrongly is better than not to think at all." -- Hypatia valued independent reasoning above all, even in an era of rising dogmatism.</blockquote>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-reading">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Recommended Reading</div>
          <ul>
            <li><em>Hypatia: The Life and Legend of an Ancient Philosopher</em> -- Edward J. Watts (2017)</li>
            <li><em>Hypatia of Alexandria</em> -- Maria Dzielska (1995)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- AL-KHWARIZMI -->
      <div class="polymath-entry">
        <div class="pm-header">
          <div class="pm-initials">AK</div>
          <div class="pm-info">
            <div class="pm-name">Al-Khwarizmi</div>
            <div class="pm-years">c. 780--850 CE</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-bio">The father of algebra. Working in Baghdad's House of Wisdom during the Islamic Golden Age, Muhammad ibn Musa al-Khwarizmi wrote the foundational text on algebra, introduced Hindu-Arabic numerals to the Islamic world, and created astronomical and trigonometric tables used for centuries. The word "algorithm" derives from the Latin form of his name.</div>
        <div class="pm-domains">
          <span class="rtag free">Mathematics</span>
          <span class="rtag must">Astronomy</span>
          <span class="rtag vid">Geography</span>
          <span class="rtag book">Algorithm Design</span>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-contributions">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Greatest Contributions</div>
          <ul>
            <li>Wrote <em>Al-Kitab al-Mukhtasar fi Hisab al-Jabr wal-Muqabala</em> -- the book that gave algebra its name</li>
            <li>Systematized the Hindu-Arabic numeral system, eventually replacing Roman numerals in Europe</li>
            <li>Produced the first reliable astronomical tables (zij) for the Islamic world</li>
            <li>Created a world map for Caliph al-Ma'mun, improving on Ptolemy's geography</li>
            <li>His name became "algorithm" -- the most fundamental concept in computer science</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-quote">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Key Insight</div>
          <blockquote>Al-Khwarizmi's genius was systematization: he took scattered methods from Babylonian, Greek, and Indian traditions and unified them into a coherent, teachable framework. The best polymaths don't just discover -- they organize knowledge so others can build on it.</blockquote>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-reading">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Recommended Reading</div>
          <ul>
            <li><em>The Algebra of Mohammed ben Musa</em> -- translated by Frederic Rosen</li>
            <li><em>The House of Wisdom</em> -- Jim Al-Khalili (2011)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- OMAR KHAYYAM -->
      <div class="polymath-entry">
        <div class="pm-header">
          <div class="pm-initials">OK</div>
          <div class="pm-info">
            <div class="pm-name">Omar Khayyam</div>
            <div class="pm-years">1048--1131 CE</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-bio">A Persian mathematician, astronomer, philosopher, and poet who achieved breakthroughs in every field he touched. Khayyam classified and solved cubic equations geometrically, reformed the Persian calendar to be more accurate than the Gregorian calendar, and wrote the <em>Rubaiyat</em> -- one of the most translated works of poetry in history.</div>
        <div class="pm-domains">
          <span class="rtag free">Mathematics</span>
          <span class="rtag must">Astronomy</span>
          <span class="rtag vid">Poetry</span>
          <span class="rtag book">Philosophy</span>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-contributions">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Greatest Contributions</div>
          <ul>
            <li>Classified and geometrically solved all types of cubic equations, five centuries before Cardano</li>
            <li>Discovered what is now called Pascal's triangle and the binomial theorem</li>
            <li>Created the Jalali calendar (1079), accurate to within one day in 5,000 years</li>
            <li>Contributed to non-Euclidean geometry by examining Euclid's parallel postulate</li>
            <li>Wrote the <em>Rubaiyat</em>, later translated by Edward FitzGerald into a Victorian sensation</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-quote">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Key Insight</div>
          <blockquote>"A loaf of bread, a jug of wine, and thou." -- Khayyam's poetry reveals a mind that balanced rigorous mathematical work with deep philosophical reflection on impermanence. He understood that the polymath life requires both discipline and wonder.</blockquote>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-reading">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Recommended Reading</div>
          <ul>
            <li><em>The Rubaiyat of Omar Khayyam</em> -- translated by Edward FitzGerald</li>
            <li><em>Omar Khayyam: Poet, Rebel, Astronomer</em> -- Hazhir Teimourian (2007)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- IBN SINA (AVICENNA) -->
      <div class="polymath-entry">
        <div class="pm-header">
          <div class="pm-initials">IS</div>
          <div class="pm-info">
            <div class="pm-name">Ibn Sina (Avicenna)</div>
            <div class="pm-years">c. 980--1037 CE</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-bio">The most influential physician and philosopher of the Islamic Golden Age. Ibn Sina wrote over 450 works, of which around 240 survive. His <em>Canon of Medicine</em> was the standard medical textbook in Europe and the Islamic world for over 600 years. He also made major contributions to Aristotelian philosophy, psychology, astronomy, and chemistry.</div>
        <div class="pm-domains">
          <span class="rtag free">Medicine</span>
          <span class="rtag must">Philosophy</span>
          <span class="rtag vid">Psychology</span>
          <span class="rtag book">Chemistry</span>
          <span class="rtag free">Astronomy</span>
          <span class="rtag must">Mathematics</span>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-contributions">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Greatest Contributions</div>
          <ul>
            <li>Wrote <em>The Canon of Medicine</em>, a 5-volume medical encyclopedia used as a textbook until the 1700s</li>
            <li>Introduced systematic experimentation and clinical trials to medicine</li>
            <li>Developed the concept of "momentum" (mail) in physics, anticipating Newton's first law</li>
            <li>Advanced Aristotelian philosophy with his distinction between essence and existence</li>
            <li>Described contagion and quarantine methods centuries before germ theory</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-quote">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Key Insight</div>
          <blockquote>"The knowledge of anything, since all things have causes, is not acquired or complete unless it is known by its causes." -- Ibn Sina's relentless pursuit of root causes across every domain is the polymath's core method: never settle for surface understanding.</blockquote>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-reading">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Recommended Reading</div>
          <ul>
            <li><em>Avicenna's Medicine</em> -- Mones Abu-Asab et al. (2013)</li>
            <li><em>Avicenna</em> -- Lenn E. Goodman (2006)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- PANEL 1: RENAISSANCE MASTERS                                  -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <div class="panel" id="p1">
    <div class="track-header">
      <div class="track-badge badge-java">1400 -- 1700 CE</div>
      <div class="track-title">Renaissance Masters</div>
      <div class="track-sub">The era when "Renaissance man" became a term. These polymaths lived during the explosion of humanism, empiricism, and the scientific revolution. They painted, built, calculated, and experimented -- often all in the same week.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="pm-grid">

      <!-- LEONARDO DA VINCI -->
      <div class="polymath-entry">
        <div class="pm-header">
          <div class="pm-initials">LV</div>
          <div class="pm-info">
            <div class="pm-name">Leonardo da Vinci</div>
            <div class="pm-years">1452--1519</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-bio">The archetypal polymath. Leonardo was a painter, sculptor, architect, musician, mathematician, engineer, inventor, anatomist, geologist, cartographer, botanist, and writer. His notebooks -- over 7,000 pages of diagrams, observations, and inventions -- reveal a mind of unparalleled curiosity. He designed flying machines, tanks, and solar power concentrators centuries before they became feasible.</div>
        <div class="pm-domains">
          <span class="rtag free">Art</span>
          <span class="rtag must">Engineering</span>
          <span class="rtag vid">Anatomy</span>
          <span class="rtag book">Architecture</span>
          <span class="rtag free">Mathematics</span>
          <span class="rtag must">Invention</span>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-contributions">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Greatest Contributions</div>
          <ul>
            <li>Painted the <em>Mona Lisa</em> and <em>The Last Supper</em>, redefining Western art</li>
            <li>Produced the most detailed anatomical drawings of his era, including the first accurate depiction of the human spine</li>
            <li>Designed flying machines (ornithopter), armored vehicles, and a robot knight</li>
            <li>Pioneered sfumato, chiaroscuro, and atmospheric perspective in painting technique</li>
            <li>Studied fluid dynamics, optics, and botany with rigorous observational method</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-quote">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Key Insight</div>
          <blockquote>"Learning never exhausts the mind." -- Leonardo's method was pure observation: he drew to understand, and understood to draw. His notebooks prove that the act of recording sharpens perception.</blockquote>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-reading">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Recommended Reading</div>
          <ul>
            <li><em>Leonardo da Vinci</em> -- Walter Isaacson (2017)</li>
            <li><em>The Notebooks of Leonardo da Vinci</em> -- translated by Jean Paul Richter</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- GALILEO GALILEI -->
      <div class="polymath-entry">
        <div class="pm-header">
          <div class="pm-initials">GG</div>
          <div class="pm-info">
            <div class="pm-name">Galileo Galilei</div>
            <div class="pm-years">1564--1642</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-bio">The father of modern observational astronomy, modern physics, and the scientific method. Galileo improved the telescope, discovered Jupiter's moons, observed Saturn's rings, and championed heliocentrism at great personal cost. His insistence on mathematical description of nature and experimental verification laid the groundwork for all modern science.</div>
        <div class="pm-domains">
          <span class="rtag free">Astronomy</span>
          <span class="rtag must">Physics</span>
          <span class="rtag vid">Mathematics</span>
          <span class="rtag book">Engineering</span>
          <span class="rtag free">Philosophy</span>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-contributions">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Greatest Contributions</div>
          <ul>
            <li>Discovered the four largest moons of Jupiter (Galilean moons), proving not everything orbits Earth</li>
            <li>Established the law of falling bodies -- all objects fall at the same rate regardless of mass</li>
            <li>Improved the telescope from 3x to 30x magnification and turned it to the sky</li>
            <li>Described the principle of relativity of motion, later central to Einstein's work</li>
            <li>Championed the heliocentric model, writing <em>Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems</em></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-quote">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Key Insight</div>
          <blockquote>"Measure what is measurable, and make measurable what is not so." -- Galileo's core principle: if you can't measure it, you can't understand it. He turned qualitative philosophy into quantitative science.</blockquote>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-reading">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Recommended Reading</div>
          <ul>
            <li><em>Galileo's Daughter</em> -- Dava Sobel (1999)</li>
            <li><em>Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems</em> -- Galileo Galilei</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ISAAC NEWTON -->
      <div class="polymath-entry">
        <div class="pm-header">
          <div class="pm-initials">IN</div>
          <div class="pm-info">
            <div class="pm-name">Isaac Newton</div>
            <div class="pm-years">1643--1727</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-bio">Widely considered the most influential scientist who ever lived. In his "annus mirabilis" (1665--1666), Newton developed calculus, discovered the laws of motion and universal gravitation, and made breakthroughs in optics -- all while Cambridge was closed for plague. He also served as Warden of the Royal Mint, wrote extensively on theology and alchemy, and invented the reflecting telescope.</div>
        <div class="pm-domains">
          <span class="rtag free">Mathematics</span>
          <span class="rtag must">Physics</span>
          <span class="rtag vid">Optics</span>
          <span class="rtag book">Astronomy</span>
          <span class="rtag free">Alchemy</span>
          <span class="rtag must">Theology</span>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-contributions">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Greatest Contributions</div>
          <ul>
            <li>Co-invented calculus (independently of Leibniz), providing the mathematical language for physics</li>
            <li>Formulated the three laws of motion and the law of universal gravitation in the <em>Principia</em></li>
            <li>Demonstrated that white light is composed of a spectrum of colors using prisms</li>
            <li>Invented the Newtonian reflecting telescope, still the basis for many modern telescopes</li>
            <li>Developed the generalized binomial theorem and Newton's method for finding roots</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-quote">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Key Insight</div>
          <blockquote>"If I have seen further, it is by standing on the shoulders of giants." -- Newton understood that polymathic achievement builds on existing knowledge. His genius was synthesizing disparate fields into unified theories.</blockquote>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-reading">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Recommended Reading</div>
          <ul>
            <li><em>Never at Rest: A Biography of Isaac Newton</em> -- Richard Westfall (1980)</li>
            <li><em>The Principia: Mathematical Principles of Natural Philosophy</em> -- Isaac Newton</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- GOTTFRIED LEIBNIZ -->
      <div class="polymath-entry">
        <div class="pm-header">
          <div class="pm-initials">GL</div>
          <div class="pm-info">
            <div class="pm-name">Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz</div>
            <div class="pm-years">1646--1716</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-bio">Perhaps the last true "universal genius." Leibniz made major contributions to mathematics, philosophy, physics, theology, law, politics, history, and engineering. He independently invented calculus (with a notation superior to Newton's that we still use today), envisioned binary arithmetic, designed mechanical calculators, and dreamed of a universal language of logic that anticipated computer science by 250 years.</div>
        <div class="pm-domains">
          <span class="rtag free">Mathematics</span>
          <span class="rtag must">Philosophy</span>
          <span class="rtag vid">Logic</span>
          <span class="rtag book">Physics</span>
          <span class="rtag free">Law</span>
          <span class="rtag must">Engineering</span>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-contributions">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Greatest Contributions</div>
          <ul>
            <li>Co-invented calculus with superior notation (dy/dx, integral sign) used universally today</li>
            <li>Developed binary arithmetic -- the foundation of all modern digital computing</li>
            <li>Created the <em>Monadology</em>, one of the most original metaphysical systems in philosophy</li>
            <li>Built the Stepped Reckoner, one of the first mechanical calculators that could multiply and divide</li>
            <li>Envisioned the <em>characteristica universalis</em> -- a formal language for all reasoning, anticipating mathematical logic and AI</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-quote">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Key Insight</div>
          <blockquote>"Nothing is more important than to see the sources of invention, which are, in my opinion, more interesting than the inventions themselves." -- Leibniz valued the process of discovery over its products, a hallmark of the polymath mindset.</blockquote>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-reading">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Recommended Reading</div>
          <ul>
            <li><em>Leibniz: An Intellectual Biography</em> -- Maria Rosa Antognazza (2009)</li>
            <li><em>Discourse on Metaphysics and Other Essays</em> -- Gottfried Leibniz</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- BLAISE PASCAL -->
      <div class="polymath-entry">
        <div class="pm-header">
          <div class="pm-initials">BP</div>
          <div class="pm-info">
            <div class="pm-name">Blaise Pascal</div>
            <div class="pm-years">1623--1662</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-bio">A child prodigy who made foundational contributions to mathematics, physics, philosophy, and theology -- all before dying at 39. Pascal invented one of the first mechanical calculators at age 19, co-founded probability theory through his correspondence with Fermat, proved the existence of vacuums against Aristotelian dogma, and wrote the <em>Pensees</em>, one of the great works of Christian philosophy.</div>
        <div class="pm-domains">
          <span class="rtag free">Mathematics</span>
          <span class="rtag must">Physics</span>
          <span class="rtag vid">Philosophy</span>
          <span class="rtag book">Theology</span>
          <span class="rtag free">Engineering</span>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-contributions">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Greatest Contributions</div>
          <ul>
            <li>Co-founded probability theory with Fermat, the basis of statistics, insurance, and machine learning</li>
            <li>Built the Pascaline, one of the first mechanical calculators, at age 19</li>
            <li>Proved atmospheric pressure exists and varies with altitude, overturning Aristotelian physics</li>
            <li>Discovered Pascal's triangle and its connection to binomial coefficients</li>
            <li>Wrote the <em>Pensees</em>, including Pascal's Wager -- the first formal decision-theoretic argument</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-quote">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Key Insight</div>
          <blockquote>"All of humanity's problems stem from man's inability to sit quietly in a room alone." -- Pascal understood that deep thought requires solitude and focus, the very conditions that enable polymathic breakthroughs.</blockquote>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-reading">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Recommended Reading</div>
          <ul>
