      <!-- LEONHARD EULER -->
      <div class="polymath-entry">
        <div class="pm-header">
          <div class="pm-initials">LE</div>
          <div class="pm-info">
            <div class="pm-name">Leonhard Euler</div>
            <div class="pm-years">1707--1783</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-bio">The most prolific mathematician in history. Euler produced over 800 papers and 90 volumes of work across pure mathematics, mechanics, optics, astronomy, and music theory. He introduced much of modern mathematical notation (e, i, pi, sigma, f(x)), continued producing groundbreaking work even after going blind, and laid foundations for graph theory, topology, and analysis.</div>
        <div class="pm-domains">
          <span class="rtag free">Mathematics</span>
          <span class="rtag must">Physics</span>
          <span class="rtag vid">Astronomy</span>
          <span class="rtag book">Music Theory</span>
          <span class="rtag free">Engineering</span>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-contributions">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Greatest Contributions</div>
          <ul>
            <li>Introduced the notation e, i, f(x), and the sigma symbol -- the modern language of mathematics</li>
            <li>Proved Euler's identity (e^(i*pi) + 1 = 0), called "the most beautiful equation in mathematics"</li>
            <li>Founded graph theory by solving the Seven Bridges of Konigsberg problem</li>
            <li>Made foundational contributions to number theory, calculus of variations, and fluid dynamics</li>
            <li>Continued producing half a page of mathematics per day even after total blindness</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-quote">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Key Insight</div>
          <blockquote>Euler's output -- averaging 800+ pages of new mathematics per year for decades -- proves that polymathic productivity is about daily practice, not sporadic inspiration. He calculated the way other people breathed: continuously, unconsciously, and prolifically.</blockquote>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-reading">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Recommended Reading</div>
          <ul>
            <li><em>Euler: The Master of Us All</em> -- William Dunham (1999)</li>
            <li><em>Dr. Euler's Fabulous Formula</em> -- Paul Nahin (2006)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- CARL FRIEDRICH GAUSS -->
      <div class="polymath-entry">
        <div class="pm-header">
          <div class="pm-initials">CG</div>
          <div class="pm-info">
            <div class="pm-name">Carl Friedrich Gauss</div>
            <div class="pm-years">1777--1855</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-bio">The "Prince of Mathematicians." A child prodigy who famously summed the integers 1 to 100 instantly at age seven, Gauss made fundamental contributions to number theory, algebra, statistics, analysis, differential geometry, geodesy, geophysics, electrostatics, astronomy, and optics. He proved the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra and developed the method of least squares, central to all modern statistics.</div>
        <div class="pm-domains">
          <span class="rtag free">Mathematics</span>
          <span class="rtag must">Astronomy</span>
          <span class="rtag vid">Physics</span>
          <span class="rtag book">Statistics</span>
          <span class="rtag free">Geodesy</span>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-contributions">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Greatest Contributions</div>
          <ul>
            <li>Wrote <em>Disquisitiones Arithmeticae</em> at age 21, reshaping number theory and algebra forever</li>
            <li>Developed the method of least squares, foundational to regression and all modern statistics</li>
            <li>Proved the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra (every polynomial has a root in the complex numbers)</li>
            <li>Discovered the Gaussian (normal) distribution -- the bell curve underpinning all of probability</li>
            <li>Made breakthroughs in non-Euclidean geometry but kept them private, decades before Bolyai and Lobachevsky published</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-quote">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Key Insight</div>
          <blockquote>"Few, but ripe." -- Gauss's motto. He only published work he considered perfect. His approach: go deep, polish relentlessly, and release only when the result is unassailable. Quality over quantity, though his quantity was still staggering.</blockquote>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-reading">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Recommended Reading</div>
          <ul>
            <li><em>The Prince of Mathematics: Carl Friedrich Gauss</em> -- M. B. W. Tent (2006)</li>
            <li><em>Gauss: A Biographical Study</em> -- W. K. Buhler (1981)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- MICHAEL FARADAY -->
      <div class="polymath-entry">
        <div class="pm-header">
          <div class="pm-initials">MF</div>
          <div class="pm-info">
            <div class="pm-name">Michael Faraday</div>
            <div class="pm-years">1791--1867</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-bio">The greatest experimental physicist in history, who rose from extreme poverty and no formal education to transform our understanding of electromagnetism. A bookbinder's apprentice who educated himself by reading the books he bound, Faraday discovered electromagnetic induction, diamagnetism, and electrolysis. His work made electric motors, generators, and transformers possible -- the entire electrical age rests on his discoveries.</div>
        <div class="pm-domains">
          <span class="rtag free">Physics</span>
          <span class="rtag must">Chemistry</span>
          <span class="rtag vid">Electromagnetism</span>
          <span class="rtag book">Science Communication</span>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-contributions">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Greatest Contributions</div>
          <ul>
            <li>Discovered electromagnetic induction -- the principle behind electric generators and transformers</li>
            <li>Invented the Faraday cage, the electric motor prototype, and the first electrical generator</li>
            <li>Established the laws of electrolysis, founding electrochemistry</li>
            <li>Introduced the concept of "fields" in physics, which Maxwell later formalized mathematically</li>
            <li>Created the Royal Institution Christmas Lectures, pioneering public science communication</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-quote">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Key Insight</div>
          <blockquote>"Nothing is too wonderful to be true, if it be consistent with the laws of nature." -- Faraday proved that lack of formal education is not a barrier. His method was pure empiricism: test everything, trust your observations, let nature teach you.</blockquote>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-reading">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Recommended Reading</div>
          <ul>
            <li><em>The Electric Life of Michael Faraday</em> -- Alan Hirshfeld (2006)</li>
