      <!-- CHARLES BABBAGE -->
      <div class="polymath-entry">
        <div class="pm-header">
          <div class="pm-initials">CB</div>
          <div class="pm-info">
            <div class="pm-name">Charles Babbage</div>
            <div class="pm-years">1791--1871</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-bio">The father of the computer. Babbage designed the Difference Engine and the Analytical Engine -- a general-purpose mechanical computer with memory, a processor, and conditional branching -- over a century before electronic computers. He also made contributions to mathematics, cryptography, economics, and mechanical engineering, and helped found the Royal Astronomical Society and the British Association for the Advancement of Science.</div>
        <div class="pm-domains">
          <span class="rtag free">Computer Science</span>
          <span class="rtag must">Mathematics</span>
          <span class="rtag vid">Engineering</span>
          <span class="rtag book">Economics</span>
          <span class="rtag free">Cryptography</span>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-contributions">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Greatest Contributions</div>
          <ul>
            <li>Designed the Analytical Engine -- the first general-purpose computing machine, with concepts of memory, CPU, and loops</li>
            <li>Built a working section of the Difference Engine for calculating mathematical tables</li>
            <li>Pioneered operations research and cost analysis in manufacturing (<em>On the Economy of Machinery and Manufactures</em>)</li>
            <li>Broke the Vigenere cipher, a major cryptographic achievement kept secret by the British government</li>
            <li>Helped establish modern scientific institutions and professionalize British science</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-quote">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Key Insight</div>
          <blockquote>"Errors using inadequate data are much less than those using no data at all." -- Babbage was obsessed with precision and automation. His vision: eliminate human error by mechanizing thought itself. He was 100 years ahead of his time.</blockquote>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-reading">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Recommended Reading</div>
          <ul>
            <li><em>The Difference Engine</em> -- Doron Swade (2001)</li>
