      <!-- HENRI POINCARE -->
      <div class="polymath-entry">
        <div class="pm-header">
          <div class="pm-initials">HP</div>
          <div class="pm-info">
            <div class="pm-name">Henri Poincare</div>
            <div class="pm-years">1854--1912</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-bio">The last mathematician to master all branches of the field in his era. Poincare founded topology, made transformative contributions to celestial mechanics, and came close to discovering special relativity before Einstein. He also wrote brilliant essays on the philosophy and psychology of mathematical creativity, describing how insights emerge from the unconscious mind after periods of incubation.</div>
        <div class="pm-domains">
          <span class="rtag free">Mathematics</span>
          <span class="rtag must">Physics</span>
          <span class="rtag vid">Philosophy</span>
          <span class="rtag book">Topology</span>
          <span class="rtag free">Celestial Mechanics</span>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-contributions">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Greatest Contributions</div>
          <ul>
            <li>Founded algebraic topology, creating entirely new mathematical tools for studying shape and space</li>
            <li>Discovered deterministic chaos in the three-body problem, founding chaos theory</li>
            <li>Formulated the Poincare conjecture, one of the seven Millennium Prize Problems (proved by Perelman in 2003)</li>
            <li>Independently derived the Lorentz transformations, coming close to special relativity</li>
            <li>Wrote <em>Science and Hypothesis</em> and <em>Science and Method</em>, classic works on the psychology of mathematical discovery</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-quote">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Key Insight</div>
          <blockquote>"It is by logic that we prove, but by intuition that we discover." -- Poincare described how breakthroughs arrive: intense focused work, followed by rest, then a sudden flash of insight from the unconscious. His theory of creativity remains influential in cognitive science.</blockquote>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-reading">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Recommended Reading</div>
          <ul>
            <li><em>Science and Hypothesis</em> -- Henri Poincare</li>
            <li><em>Henri Poincare: A Scientific Biography</em> -- Jeremy Gray (2012)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- JOHN VON NEUMANN -->
      <div class="polymath-entry">
        <div class="pm-header">
          <div class="pm-initials">JN</div>
          <div class="pm-info">
            <div class="pm-name">John von Neumann</div>
            <div class="pm-years">1903--1957</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-bio">Widely regarded as the greatest intellect of the 20th century. Von Neumann made fundamental contributions to mathematics, physics, computer science, economics, and game theory. He designed the architecture still used in virtually every modern computer, co-developed the atomic bomb, founded game theory, formalized quantum mechanics, and invented cellular automata. Colleagues described his mental speed as "superhuman."</div>
        <div class="pm-domains">
          <span class="rtag free">Mathematics</span>
          <span class="rtag must">Computer Science</span>
          <span class="rtag vid">Physics</span>
          <span class="rtag book">Economics</span>
          <span class="rtag free">Game Theory</span>
          <span class="rtag must">Nuclear Science</span>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-contributions">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Greatest Contributions</div>
          <ul>
            <li>Designed the von Neumann architecture -- the stored-program computer model used in virtually all modern machines</li>
            <li>Co-founded game theory with Oskar Morgenstern, publishing <em>Theory of Games and Economic Behavior</em></li>
            <li>Provided the rigorous mathematical foundation for quantum mechanics</li>
            <li>Invented cellular automata and the concept of self-replicating machines</li>
            <li>Made critical contributions to the Manhattan Project (implosion lens design) and Monte Carlo simulation methods</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-quote">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Key Insight</div>
          <blockquote>"If people do not believe that mathematics is simple, it is only because they do not realize how complicated life is." -- Von Neumann's approach: mathematics is not a specialty, it is the universal language. Every problem, from economics to warfare to biology, yields to mathematical formalization.</blockquote>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-reading">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Recommended Reading</div>
          <ul>
            <li><em>The Man from the Future</em> -- Ananyo Bhattacharya (2022)</li>
            <li><em>Theory of Games and Economic Behavior</em> -- John von Neumann and Oskar Morgenstern</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ALAN TURING -->
      <div class="polymath-entry">
        <div class="pm-header">
          <div class="pm-initials">AT</div>
          <div class="pm-info">
            <div class="pm-name">Alan Turing</div>
            <div class="pm-years">1912--1954</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-bio">The father of computer science and artificial intelligence. Turing formalized the concept of computation itself with the Turing machine, cracked the Enigma code at Bletchley Park (arguably shortening WWII by two years), proposed the Turing Test for machine intelligence, and pioneered mathematical biology with his work on morphogenesis. His life was tragically cut short after prosecution for homosexuality.</div>
        <div class="pm-domains">
          <span class="rtag free">Computer Science</span>
          <span class="rtag must">Mathematics</span>
          <span class="rtag vid">Cryptography</span>
          <span class="rtag book">AI</span>
          <span class="rtag free">Biology</span>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-contributions">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Greatest Contributions</div>
          <ul>
            <li>Defined the Turing machine (1936), formalizing the concept of "algorithm" and proving the limits of computation</li>
            <li>Led the effort to crack the Enigma cipher at Bletchley Park, helping shorten WWII significantly</li>
            <li>Proposed the Turing Test (1950), the foundational concept for artificial intelligence</li>
            <li>Developed mathematical models of morphogenesis, explaining how patterns form in biology</li>
            <li>Designed the ACE computer, one of the first stored-program electronic computers</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-quote">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Key Insight</div>
          <blockquote>"We can only see a short distance ahead, but we can see plenty there that needs to be done." -- Turing's genius was asking the right questions: What is computation? Can machines think? Why do leopards have spots? Each question opened an entire field.</blockquote>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-reading">
          <div class="pm-section-title">Recommended Reading</div>
          <ul>
            <li><em>Alan Turing: The Enigma</em> -- Andrew Hodges (1983)</li>
