  <!-- PANEL 5: THE POLYMATH PATTERN                                 -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <div class="panel" id="p5">
    <div class="track-header">
      <div class="track-badge badge-build">Meta-Analysis</div>
      <div class="track-title">The Polymath Pattern</div>
      <div class="track-sub">What do Aristotle, Leonardo, von Neumann, and Feynman have in common? Beneath the surface of their individual brilliance lies a set of recurring patterns, habits, and cognitive strategies. Study these patterns. They are learnable.</div>
    </div>

    <!-- COMMON TRAITS -->
    <div class="pm-analysis-section">
      <div class="pm-analysis-title">Common Traits Across All Eras</div>
      <div class="pm-analysis-grid">
        <div class="pm-trait-card">
          <div class="pm-trait-num">01</div>
          <div class="pm-trait-name">Insatiable Curiosity</div>
          <div class="pm-trait-desc">Every polymath shared an almost pathological need to understand how things work. Leonardo dissected cadavers to understand muscles. Feynman picked locks to understand security. Shannon built juggling robots for fun. This curiosity is not passive -- it is an active, relentless drive to pull apart and reassemble every phenomenon they encounter.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-trait-card">
          <div class="pm-trait-num">02</div>
          <div class="pm-trait-name">First-Principles Thinking</div>
          <div class="pm-trait-desc">Polymaths don't accept inherited assumptions. Galileo questioned Aristotle. Musk questioned rocket pricing. Turing questioned what "computation" even means. They strip problems to their fundamental components, then rebuild from ground truth. This is the cognitive habit that enables domain transfer: if you understand principles, you can apply them anywhere.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-trait-card">
          <div class="pm-trait-num">03</div>
          <div class="pm-trait-name">Prolific Note-Taking & Externalization</div>
          <div class="pm-trait-desc">Leonardo's 7,200+ pages of notebooks. Euler's 800 papers. Gauss's mathematical diary. Feynman's notebooks. Polymaths externalize their thinking obsessively. Writing is not just record-keeping -- it is thinking. The act of writing forces clarity, reveals gaps, and creates a "second brain" that compounds over time. Every polymath was also a prolific writer.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-trait-card">
          <div class="pm-trait-num">04</div>
          <div class="pm-trait-name">Cross-Pollination</div>
          <div class="pm-trait-desc">The defining move: importing ideas from one field into another. Hinton brought physics (Boltzmann distributions) into AI. Simons brought mathematics into finance. Shannon brought Boolean logic into circuit design. Poincare brought topology into celestial mechanics. The breakthrough almost always comes from applying a mature tool from Field A to an unsolved problem in Field B.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-trait-card">
          <div class="pm-trait-num">05</div>
          <div class="pm-trait-name">High Tolerance for Confusion</div>
          <div class="pm-trait-desc">Polymaths spend most of their time confused -- and they are comfortable with it. When entering a new domain, they accept being a beginner. Von Neumann moved from pure math to physics to economics to computing, each time starting from ignorance. The willingness to be confused is what separates polymaths from specialists: specialists avoid confusion, polymaths seek it.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-trait-card">
          <div class="pm-trait-num">06</div>
          <div class="pm-trait-name">Playfulness & Tinkering</div>
          <div class="pm-trait-desc">Shannon juggled and built gadgets. Feynman played bongos and cracked safes. Franklin flew kites. Leonardo sketched imaginary machines. Polymaths maintain a childlike sense of play that keeps their creativity alive. The line between "serious work" and "play" is invisible to them because both are expressions of curiosity.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- T-SHAPE MODEL -->
    <div class="pm-analysis-section">
      <div class="pm-analysis-title">The Expertise Evolution: T to Pi to Comb</div>
      <div class="pm-shape-model">
        <div class="pm-shape-card">
          <div class="pm-shape-icon">T</div>
          <div class="pm-shape-name">T-Shaped (Specialist+)</div>
          <div class="pm-shape-desc">Deep expertise in one domain with broad awareness of adjacent fields. This is where most experts stop. Examples: a mathematician who reads popular science, an engineer who appreciates art. The "T" has one deep vertical bar (your specialty) and a horizontal bar (general knowledge).</div>
          <div class="pm-shape-example">Example: A machine learning engineer who also understands product design.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-shape-card">
          <div class="pm-shape-icon">&Pi;</div>
          <div class="pm-shape-name">Pi-Shaped (Dual Expert)</div>
          <div class="pm-shape-desc">Deep expertise in two domains with the horizontal bar of general knowledge connecting them. This is where cross-pollination begins. The combination of two deep skills creates unique insights that neither domain alone could produce. Most successful founders and innovators are Pi-shaped.</div>
          <div class="pm-shape-example">Example: Hassabis (neuroscience + AI), Simons (mathematics + finance).</div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-shape-card">
          <div class="pm-shape-icon">IIII</div>
          <div class="pm-shape-name">Comb-Shaped (Polymath)</div>
          <div class="pm-shape-desc">Deep expertise in three or more domains with a broad horizontal bar connecting them all. This is the full polymath. The "comb" has multiple deep teeth (domains of genuine competence) connected by a broad base of general knowledge. Each additional deep skill multiplies -- not adds -- the possible connections.</div>
          <div class="pm-shape-example">Example: Von Neumann (math + physics + CS + economics), Feynman (physics + math + teaching + biology + art).</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pm-evolution-note">
        <strong>The key insight:</strong> You don't need to be comb-shaped from day one. Start with T (go deep in one thing). Then develop a second spike (Pi). Then a third. Each new depth takes less time because your learning infrastructure -- note-taking, first-principles thinking, pattern recognition -- transfers. Leonardo didn't start as a polymath; he started as a painter's apprentice.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- DAILY ROUTINES -->
    <div class="pm-analysis-section">
      <div class="pm-analysis-title">Daily Routines Comparison</div>
      <div class="pm-routine-grid">
        <div class="pm-routine-card">
          <div class="pm-routine-name">Leonardo da Vinci</div>
          <div class="pm-routine-desc">Worked in intense bursts across multiple projects. Rose early to sketch and observe. Alternated between painting, engineering, and anatomical study. Kept notebooks always at hand. Walked extensively for observation and thinking. No fixed schedule -- driven by curiosity and commission deadlines.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-routine-card">
          <div class="pm-routine-name">Isaac Newton</div>
          <div class="pm-routine-desc">Legendary for obsessive focus. During his <em>annus mirabilis</em>, he barely ate or slept. Often forgot meals. Worked alone for extended periods. His method was sustained, deep concentration on a single problem until it cracked. He would then pivot to an entirely different domain.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-routine-card">
          <div class="pm-routine-name">Leonhard Euler</div>
          <div class="pm-routine-desc">Wrote mathematics every day without exception, producing roughly half a page of finished work daily. Worked surrounded by his 13 children. Maintained this output even after going completely blind, dictating to assistants. His routine was pure consistency -- no bursts, just relentless daily output.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-routine-card">
          <div class="pm-routine-name">Benjamin Franklin</div>
          <div class="pm-routine-desc">Famous for his structured daily schedule: rose at 5am, asked "What good shall I do this day?", worked in focused blocks, reserved evenings for review. Tracked 13 virtues in a weekly chart. His system was the original "habit tracker" -- 250 years before apps existed.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-routine-card">
          <div class="pm-routine-name">Richard Feynman</div>
          <div class="pm-routine-desc">Alternated between intense work and deliberate play. Frequented strip clubs to sketch. Played bongos in a samba band. His "unproductive" activities were actually incubation periods. He worked on physics problems on and off all day, with no clear boundary between work and life.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-routine-card">
          <div class="pm-routine-name">John von Neumann</div>
          <div class="pm-routine-desc">Threw lavish parties and maintained an active social life (unusual for a mathematician). Worked in noisy environments and claimed he couldn't focus in silence. Solved problems while driving or at parties. His routine was scattered by external standards but internally consistent -- his brain never stopped.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- CROSS-DOMAIN CONNECTIONS -->
    <div class="pm-analysis-section">
      <div class="pm-analysis-title">How They Connected Their Domains</div>
      <div class="pm-connections-list">
        <div class="pm-connection-item">
          <div class="pm-connection-from">Leonardo: Art &rarr; Anatomy</div>
          <div class="pm-connection-desc">To paint the human body more accurately, Leonardo dissected over 30 cadavers and produced the most detailed anatomical drawings of his era. The artistic drive for realism produced genuine scientific discovery.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-connection-item">
          <div class="pm-connection-from">Shannon: Mathematics &rarr; Circuit Design</div>
          <div class="pm-connection-desc">Shannon's master's thesis proved that Boolean algebra could be implemented in electrical circuits. This single insight -- connecting abstract logic to physical switches -- is the foundation of all digital computing.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-connection-item">
          <div class="pm-connection-from">Hinton: Physics &rarr; Neural Networks</div>
          <div class="pm-connection-desc">Hinton imported the Boltzmann distribution from statistical physics to create Boltzmann machines -- neural networks that learn by settling into energy minima. This physics metaphor produced a generation of breakthroughs in machine learning.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-connection-item">
          <div class="pm-connection-from">Poincare: Topology &rarr; Celestial Mechanics</div>
          <div class="pm-connection-desc">Poincare invented algebraic topology to study the qualitative behavior of planetary orbits. He didn't solve the three-body problem -- he proved it was unsolvable and discovered chaos, founding an entirely new science in the process.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-connection-item">
          <div class="pm-connection-from">Feynman: Visualization &rarr; Particle Physics</div>
          <div class="pm-connection-desc">Feynman diagrams transformed quantum electrodynamics from impenetrable mathematics into intuitive visual representations. He brought an artist's instinct for spatial thinking into the most abstract domain in physics.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-connection-item">
          <div class="pm-connection-from">Simons: Differential Geometry &rarr; Financial Markets</div>
          <div class="pm-connection-desc">Simons applied mathematical pattern recognition (hidden Markov models, statistical anomalies) from his pure math background to find structure in financial data that economists couldn't see, because economists weren't looking with the right tools.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ACTIONABLE LESSONS -->
    <div class="pm-analysis-section">
      <div class="pm-analysis-title">Actionable Lessons for Modern Polymath Aspirants</div>
      <div class="pm-lessons-list">
        <div class="pm-lesson-item">
          <div class="pm-lesson-num">01</div>
          <div class="pm-lesson-content">
            <div class="pm-lesson-title">Go Deep First, Then Wide</div>
            <div class="pm-lesson-desc">Don't try to learn everything at once. Master one field deeply enough to contribute. Then use the learning infrastructure you built -- note-taking, first-principles thinking, rapid prototyping -- to accelerate learning in the next field. Depth precedes breadth.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-lesson-item">
          <div class="pm-lesson-num">02</div>
          <div class="pm-lesson-content">
            <div class="pm-lesson-title">Keep a Commonplace Book / Zettelkasten</div>
            <div class="pm-lesson-desc">Every polymath externalized their thinking. Start a system for capturing, connecting, and retrieving ideas across domains. Use Obsidian, Notion, or physical notebooks. The key is cross-referencing: link ideas from different fields. This is where serendipitous connections emerge.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-lesson-item">
          <div class="pm-lesson-num">03</div>
          <div class="pm-lesson-content">
            <div class="pm-lesson-title">Practice Deliberate Transfer</div>
            <div class="pm-lesson-desc">When you learn a concept in one field, immediately ask: "Where else does this pattern appear?" Gradient descent in ML is hill-climbing in optimization is natural selection in biology is simulated annealing in physics. The concept is one; the applications are infinite.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-lesson-item">
          <div class="pm-lesson-num">04</div>
          <div class="pm-lesson-content">
            <div class="pm-lesson-title">Read Outside Your Field Every Day</div>
            <div class="pm-lesson-desc">Dedicate 30 minutes daily to reading outside your primary domain. If you're a programmer, read biology. If you're a mathematician, read history. If you're an artist, read physics. The polymaths didn't silo their reading -- their breakthroughs came from unexpected combinations.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-lesson-item">
          <div class="pm-lesson-num">05</div>
          <div class="pm-lesson-content">
            <div class="pm-lesson-title">Build Things Constantly</div>
            <div class="pm-lesson-desc">Feynman: "What I cannot create, I do not understand." Don't just read about domains -- build in them. Write code. Run experiments. Make art. Build circuits. The act of creation forces understanding in a way that passive study never can. Ship something in every domain you enter.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-lesson-item">
          <div class="pm-lesson-num">06</div>
          <div class="pm-lesson-content">
            <div class="pm-lesson-title">Teach What You Learn</div>
            <div class="pm-lesson-desc">Feynman taught. Euler wrote textbooks. Gauss mentored. Franklin published. Teaching forces you to fill gaps in your understanding and creates a feedback loop that accelerates mastery. Write blog posts, make videos, mentor others -- the polymath who teaches becomes the polymath who truly understands.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-lesson-item">
          <div class="pm-lesson-num">07</div>
          <div class="pm-lesson-content">
            <div class="pm-lesson-title">Embrace the Adjacent Possible</div>
            <div class="pm-lesson-desc">Don't plan your polymathic journey too rigidly. Follow your curiosity to the "adjacent possible" -- the domains just beyond your current knowledge that share structural similarities. From math, the adjacent possible includes physics, CS, and economics. From biology, it includes chemistry, medicine, and AI. Each step opens new doors.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-lesson-item">
          <div class="pm-lesson-num">08</div>
          <div class="pm-lesson-content">
            <div class="pm-lesson-title">Protect Unstructured Thinking Time</div>
            <div class="pm-lesson-desc">Poincare's breakthroughs came during walks. Newton's came during plague isolation. Feynman's came while playing bongos. Schedule time with no agenda, no screens, and no inputs. This is when your unconscious mind connects the dots between domains. The modern polymath's greatest enemy is not ignorance -- it is distraction.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>

